WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.695
Each node of your three-node cluster holds 40 gigabytes, and the disks are filling up.

2
00:00:07.695 --> 00:00:09.903
You add a fourth node to take a share.

3
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:15.854
Each node owns many small ranges of the ring, one ending at each of its tokens.

4
00:00:15.854 --> 00:00:18.112
Real nodes have sixteen tokens.

5
00:00:18.112 --> 00:00:19.059
We draw four.

6
00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:25.716
The new node needs two settings: the cluster's name, which must match, and a seed, a node it calls first.

7
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.099
It starts and gossips its way in.

8
00:00:28.099 --> 00:00:32.697
Within seconds every node knows it, and it knows every node's tokens.

9
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:38.234
Now it picks its own sixteen tokens, placed by an allocator to even out the load.

10
00:00:38.234 --> 00:00:40.935
In the status table, it shows as joining.

11
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:47.281
Its new ranges still sit on the old nodes, so they stream that data to it, about 30 gigabytes.

12
00:00:47.281 --> 00:00:51.728
Meanwhile it takes new writes for those ranges, but serves no reads.

13
00:00:51.933 --> 00:00:55.177
When streaming ends, it turns UN: up and normal.

14
00:00:55.177 --> 00:00:58.624
Now those ranges are its own, and it answers reads.

15
00:00:58.833 --> 00:01:00.748
But look at the load column.

16
00:01:00.748 --> 00:01:03.688
The old nodes still hold 40 gigabytes each.

17
00:01:03.688 --> 00:01:05.466
Streaming copied the data.

18
00:01:05.466 --> 00:01:07.585
Nothing deleted the old copies.

19
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:11.027
No read will ever ask them for those rows again.

20
00:01:11.027 --> 00:01:16.539
So you run nodetool cleanup on each old node, one at a time, and each drops to 30.

21
00:01:16.767 --> 00:01:19.579
Removing a node is the same story backwards.

22
00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:23.543
You run decommission on the new node, and it shows as leaving.

23
00:01:23.733 --> 00:01:28.607
It streams each of its ranges to the node that owns it next, then leaves the ring.

24
00:01:28.607 --> 00:01:33.005
The others are back to 40 gigabytes each, and there's nothing to clean up.

25
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.572
So a joining node gossips in, takes its tokens and streams its ranges.

26
00:01:37.572 --> 00:01:40.632
The old owners clean up what they no longer need.

27
00:01:40.632 --> 00:01:42.756
And you change one node at a time.
